"Surah al-Muddaththir begins with an address meaning \"the one enveloped in his cloak.\" This word is originally \"mutadaththir,\" meaning one who wraps himself in an outer garment (dithar). It is narrated that the cloak the Prophet wrapped himself in was made of velvet. According to 'Ikrimah, this means the one enveloped in prophethood and spiritual perfections. This address conveys that the time for sleep and comfort has passed, and that the time has come to awaken, proclaim the truth, and endure hardships. Verse 2 contains the command \"arise and warn,\" while verse 3 contains the command \"and magnify your Lord.\" According to a narration, when this verse was revealed, the Messenger of Allah said \"Allahu akbar,\" and Khadijah also rejoiced by uttering the takbir. The letter \"fa\" signifies attachment to the preceding command.\n\nIn verse 4, the command \"keep your garments clean\" encompasses both physical and spiritual cleanliness. The word \"thiyab\" (garments) here has been interpreted as a metaphor for the soul, the heart, or moral character. Furthermore, it is also noted that \"thiyab\" denotes the community that surrounds a person, which points to the verse \"and warn your nearest kinsfolk.\" In verse 5, \"rujz\" or \"rijz\" means impurity and punishment, as well as idols and statues. In verse 6, \"do not confer favor\" is explained in two ways: reproaching someone with a favor, or doing good while expecting something in return. Verse 7 gives the command \"and for your Lord, be patient.\"\n\nVerses 8–10 describe the blowing of the Trumpet: \"When the horn is blown, that day will be a difficult day for the disbelievers.\" Naqur means horn. The difficulty of that day is a hardship that steadily increases and never becomes easy. Verses 11–17 contain a warning specifically concerning al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah: \"Leave Me with him whom I created alone.\" He was granted abundant wealth, sons present by his side, and vast means, yet he desired even more. Saying \"Nay!\" Allah declares that He will drive him up a steep ascent (Sa'ud). Sa'ud is a mountain of fire in Hell; the disbeliever climbs it for seventy years and then falls.\n\nVerses 18–25 describe Walid's obstinate attitude: He thought and plotted, frowned and scowled, turned his back and waxed proud. Concerning the Quran, he said it was \"magic handed down from of old\" or \"dazzling magic.\" According to the details of the incident, Walid listened to the Quran from the Prophet and was moved, but under pressure from Quraysh, he rejected it. He reached his verdict by saying, \"This is nothing but the word of a human being.\"\n\nVerses 26–31 introduce the Hell of Saqar: \"I will cast him into Saqar.\" Saqar means scorching; it leaves nothing remaining and spares nothing. \"Incessantly scorching the skins\" (lawwahah). Over it are nineteen (angels). Allah made the keepers of the Fire angels and made their number a trial for the disbelievers. Abu Jahl's objection or the questioning of the Jews is narrated as the occasion of revelation. The expression \"none knows the soldiers of your Lord except Him\" emphasizes that the mystery of this number requires faith.\n\nIn verses 32–39, Allah swears by the moon, the night, and the morning, declaring that Saqar is one of the greatest calamities and that it has come to warn humanity. \"Every soul is bound to what it has earned, except the companions of the right.\" They are in Paradise and ask the criminals: \"What led you into Saqar?\"\n\nIn verses 40–56, the answer of the criminals is related: \"We were not of those who prayed, we did not feed the poor, we engaged in vain discourse with those who indulged, and we denied the Day of Recompense, until death finally came.\" The intercession of intercessors will no longer benefit them. They turn away from the Quran; they are like frightened wild asses. Each of them wants unrolled scrolls given to him, but they do not fear the Hereafter. Yet the Quran is an admonition; whoever wills may take heed, but they cannot take heed unless Allah wills. He is the Protector and the Forgiver.\n\nThe oaths sworn by the moon, the night, and the morning in verse 34 point not only to these entities, but also to the dawning of the light of Prophethood from the darkness of ignorance (jahiliyyah) and the approach of the sun of Islam.\n\nSaqar (Hell) is one of the momentous matters; it is recounted to warn people. Every soul is pledged to its earning; whoever does good is saved, and whoever does evil suffers its punishment.\n\nHowever, those who are given their record of deeds from the right (ashab al-yamin) are an exception to this general rule. Rather than being tied solely to their own earnings, they attain higher ranks through Allah's grace. This is a grace that, like Prophethood, is not acquired through effort. Ashab al-yamin has been interpreted in two ways: those who are on the right side in destiny, or those who remain true to their covenant.\n\nThe companions of the right in Paradise ask the criminals, 'What led you into Saqar?' The criminals say: 'We were not of those who prayed, we did not feed the poor, we joined with those who engaged in vain pursuits, and we denied the Day of Recompense. We were like this until death came upon us.'\n\nThe intercession of intercessors will no longer benefit them, because they died in disbelief. Those who turn away from Allah's admonition flee from a lion like frightened wild asses. Yet they are fleeing from salvation and running toward ruin.\n\nThey demand that pages specifically for them be sent down, but the real issue is that they do not fear the Hereafter. The Quran is an admonition; whoever wills reflects. Yet no one reflects unless Allah wills. He alone is the One to be feared and the One to forgive. In hadiths, it is narrated that Allah said, 'I am the One to be feared and the One to forgive.'"